---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { base } from "../../lib/site";
---
<Layout
  title="Open bibliography"
  description="Reported public sources behind zhao-langxi essays: disclosure rules, press on AI concierges, and HRI papers on interruption and shared space."
  canonical="https://zhao-langxi.github.io/zhao-langxi/research/bibliography.html"
  current="research"
>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <header>
      <p>Research and methods</p>
      <h1>Open bibliography</h1>
      <p>
        These are <strong>Reported</strong> sources I actually opened. A listing is not proof that a system works, that people trust it, or that I did fieldwork inside the institution.
      </p>
    </header>
    <article>
      <h2>Disclosure and labelling</h2>
      <ul class="resource-list">
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3786156.3788574" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vesala, Rauti, Rajapaksha, Hjerppe, and Heino (ACM 2026)</a></h2>
          <p>
            <em>Examining Transparency Requirements for AI-Based Chatbots and Websites.</em>
            RAIE 2026. They read EU AI Act Article 50 as requiring notice at the latest at first interaction, then checked Finnish public-service chatbots. Only one of twelve told the user it was AI before the chat began.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj/eng" rel="noopener noreferrer">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 · EU AI Act</a></h2>
          <p>
            Official text. Article 50 is the transparency rule Vesala et al. apply.
            Plain-language note: <a href="https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-50" rel="noopener noreferrer">Article 50 service desk</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://www.pcpd.org.hk/english/news_events/media_statements/press_20240611.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hong Kong PCPD (11 June 2024)</a></h2>
          <p>
            Press note for <em>Artificial Intelligence: Model Personal Data Protection Framework</em>.
            Asks for prominent disclosure of AI use, explainability where impact is high, and channels for correction, human intervention, and opt-out.
            PDF: <a href="https://www.pcpd.org.hk/english/resources_centre/publications/files/ai_protection_framework.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">ai_protection_framework.pdf</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/zhengceku/2022-12/12/content_5731431.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">China · Deep Synthesis Provisions (2022)</a></h2>
          <p>
            Official Chinese text. Article 17 asks for a conspicuous label when dialogue, voice, or face services may confuse the public.
            English translation: <a href="https://www.chinalawtranslate.com/en/deep-synthesis/" rel="noopener noreferrer">China Law Translate</a>.
            Library of Congress note: <a href="https://www.loc.gov/item/global-legal-monitor/2023-04-25/china-provisions-on-deep-synthesis-technology-enter-into-effect/" rel="noopener noreferrer">entered into effect 10 January 2023</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Social responses to machines</h2>
      <ul class="resource-list">
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/0022-4537.00153" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nass and Moon (2000)</a></h2>
          <p>
            <em>Machines and Mindlessness: Social Responses to Computers.</em>
            <cite>Journal of Social Issues</cite> 56(1). People apply politeness, gender stereotypes, and reciprocity to computers even while saying the machine is not a person. Useful for Digital Humans: a face can trigger social habits without earning trust.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Shared space and interruption</h2>
      <ul class="resource-list">
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.06383" rel="noopener noreferrer">Banerjee, Silva, Feigh, and Chernova (2018)</a></h2>
          <p>
            <em>Effects of Interruptibility-Aware Robot Behavior.</em>
            Open preprint. A robot that waits for a better moment is rated more considerate, and the robot wastes less time. The paper studies the robot interrupting the person. The open question on this site is the reverse: when can the person interrupt the robot.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3613904.3642082" rel="noopener noreferrer">Babel, Welsch, Miller, Hock, Thellman, and Ziemke (CHI 2024)</a></h2>
          <p>
            <em>A Robot Jumping the Queue.</em>
            Elevator conflict study (N = 335). A robot that commands priority or enters first is less accepted unless people were primed to expect an assertive machine. Everyday politeness is not optional decoration.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.02279" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hetherington, Lee, Haase, Croft, and Van der Loos (IROS 2021)</a></h2>
          <p>
            <em>Mobile Robot Yielding Cues for Human-Robot Spatial Interaction.</em>
            Open preprint. Doorway and bottleneck cues with 102 online viewers. A retreating robot was the most socially acceptable yield.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/iros40897.2019.8967862" rel="noopener noreferrer">Thomas and Vaughan (IROS 2019)</a></h2>
          <p>
            <em>Right of Way, Assertiveness and Social Recognition in Human-Robot Doorway Interaction.</em>
            People were more likely to respect a robot’s right of way when the robot communicated more clearly. Lab and doorway study, not a street deployment. IEEE copy may sit behind a paywall.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Public record · the concierge idea</h2>
      <p>Press coverage of Whitney Wolfe Herd’s public remarks. Paraphrased on <a href={`${base}question.html`}>The concierge question</a>. Not a product result.</p>
      <ul class="resource-list">
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/10/bumble-founder-whitney-wolfe-herd-says-ai-could-date-for-you.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">CNBC (10 May 2024)</a></h2>
          <p>Private coaching, dating fatigue, and the concierge that might talk to other concierges first.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/internet/ai-personas-are-future-dating-bumble-founder-says-many-arent-buying-rcna151738" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBC News (11 May 2024)</a></h2>
          <p>Same remarks, plus public skepticism.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://fortune.com/2024/05/10/bumbles-whitney-wolfe-herd-dating-concierge-artificial-intelligence/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fortune (10 May 2024)</a></h2>
          <p>The city-sized first pass and “top three” example.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/whitney-wolfe-herd-bumble-ai-398779bb" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wall Street Journal</a></h2>
          <p>Reported Bee assistant and emotionally informed onboarding. Paywalled.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h2><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2023/09/28/bumble-ceo-whitney-wolfe-herd-shares-how-ai-will-supercharge-love-and-relationships/" rel="noopener noreferrer">TechCrunch (28 September 2023)</a></h2>
          <p>Earlier public comments on keeping AI pointed toward human relationships rather than replacing them.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Institutional catalogues</h2>
      <p>Public listings used in <a href={`${base}places.html`}>Places and evidence</a>. Evidence of what an institution published, not of trust.</p>
      <ul class="resource-list">
        <li>
